  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 126 PM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nScattered high clouds over NE Nebraska this afternoon. Otherwise\nits a mostly sunny day with highs forecast to get into the upper\n70s to low 80s across most of our area this afternoon. Looking\nat the broader pattern we have a broad ridge over the Central\nPlains with troughs over California and the Appalachians.  \n\nOvernight tonight we'll see the LLJ ramp up across central into\nnortheast Nebraska. With the nose of the LLJ right along the SD\nstate line, any potential showers/storms should stay in South\nDakota, but we do have slight-chance PoPs right near the state\nline. These stronger winds out of the south-southwest hang\naround into the day on Friday, mixing down to the surface mid-\nmorning. We'll see fairly strong winds through Friday afternoon\ngusting 30-45 mph in NE Nebraska, 20-30 mph across SE Nebraska \nand SW Iowa. These winds are associated with that western \ntrough shifting eastward with a developing low pressure system\nover eastern Montana.\n\nThis deepening low tracks northeast into NW North Dakota and \nManitoba/Saskatchewan, but brings a trailing cold front into our\narea on Saturday. This front will stall, hanging around through\nSunday with cooler temperatures north of the boundary, and highs\nstaying in the mid-70s to low-80s south. Showers and storms will\nbe possible along the front over NE Nebraska starting after \nmidnight Saturday into Saturday morning. While initially precip \nwill have to overcome a substantial amount of low-level dry air \nearly Saturday morning, these chances increase Saturday evening \ninto Sunday as we see persistent moisture pooling along the\nboundary overcome this dry air. \n\nModels continue to trend later with the arrival of the upper-\nlevel trough which will push this frontal boundary through. Rain\nchances now aren't likely to spread across the rest of our area\nuntil late Sunday or early Monday. Cooler, rainy weather \nappears likely to hang around through Tuesday clearing out \nWednesday as the upper-level trough brings a surface low \nthrough. \n\nWeak ridging builds back in later next week, lowering\nprecip chances. Ensembles show lower confidence in the forecast\ngoing into the latter half of the week due to timing differences\nin the passage of the upper-level trough as well as in the\narrival and amplitude of the weak ridge moving in later in the\nweek. Temperature spreads for highs go from mid 50s to low 70s\nfor the Omaha area (25-75 percentile). The current forecast\nacross our area is near the ensemble mean.",
